WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.860
Annual parallax uses Earth's orbit as a change in

00:00:02.860 --> 00:00:06.040
viewpoint. Six months apart, Earth is on opposite sides

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:08.840
of the Sun. A nearby star appears to shift

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:11.520
against more distant stars. The star does not jump

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:15.160
sideways in space. Move the same star farther away,

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:18.560
and its angular shift becomes smaller. The parallax angle

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:23.050
is half the total six-month displacement. With parallax in

00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:26.520
arcseconds, distance in parsecs equals one divided by parallax.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:29.380
A parallax of one tenth of an arcsecond means

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:33.312
ten parsecs. Twice the distance gives half the parallax.
